ctlsock: the homing overshoot does not die at the guest's clamp — it sits in
the DEVICE's accumulator and cancels the very next travel

Applies AFTER mame-ctlsock-open-loop-gain.patch (same file, same branch).

THE BUG. The open-loop first-MOVEA branch homes and travels in one breath:

	move_paced(-(18 * m_move_step), -(18 * m_move_step));   // 2160 counts
	move_paced_travel_px(x, y);

with the standing comment "the overshoot must die against the guest's edge
clamp before the real move starts". It does not. The clamp that stops the
ARROW lives in the mouse card's firmware, several stages downstream of the
accumulator that nets our counts. On the Apple II Mouse Card
(src/devices/bus/a2bus/mouse.cpp, update_axis()):

	m_count[AXIS] += diff;        // signed, unbounded, NETS
	m_last[AXIS]   = new_m;
	if (m_count[AXIS]) { toggle CLK; m_count[AXIS] -= sgn; }   // ONE per read

m_count drains one unit per MCU port-B read. A 2160-count slam therefore
parks m_count at roughly -2000 for as long as the drain takes, and the travel
we append microseconds later is added to that number, not to the arrow. The
arrow moves by the NET, and the net is still leftward, so the first target's
whole travel is spent unwinding a slam that had already reached the corner.
An open loop's only origin is its belief, so the deficit never clears: every
later target inherits it exactly.

MEASURED (apple2e enhanced, Apple II Mouse Card slot 4, Dazzle Draw canvas,
golden.sta, PTR_MOD=256, SCREEN=1024x768, GAIN 1.547/1.674, arrow tip located
in the framebuffer, x>=10 and 72<=y<640):

  (1) There is no debt once the queue has drained. Home with a paced
      MOVE -1200 -1200, settle, then step +50 counts per axis:

	counts   50  100  150  200  250  300  350
	tip x    82  163  243  324  404  485  565     (81,80,81,80,81,80)
	tip y    92  180  268  356  444  532  620     (88 flat)

      The FIRST +50 moves the arrow. Zero counts are consumed before motion,
      and the response is dead linear. Homing itself is not the problem.

  (2) The slam and the travel back to back lose the travel ENTIRELY, at every
      slam size; a settle between them lands it exactly. Pre-homed to the
      corner each time, then MOVE -slam, [gap], MOVE 194 119:

	slam    gap=none        gap=settle
	 700    no arrow        (300, 204)
	1200    no arrow        (304, 204)
	2160    no arrow        (300, 204)

  (3) It is the device accumulator netting, not a module window merge. The
      same back-to-back pair issued in the INTERIOR, nowhere near a clamp
      (from tip (603,408), MOVE -300 -200 then MOVE 194 119) lands at
      (446, 272) — the arithmetic net, -106 counts * 1.547 = -164 px, to
      within 10 px. Nothing is lost when there is no clamp to hide it: the
      counts net, and at the corner the net is invisible.

  (4) Whole-run confirmation through MOVEA, pre-homed, five targets:

	command          issuedX  issuedY   tip        err
	MOVEA 300 200      2354     2279    none        -        (2160 slam + 194)
	MOVEA 700 500       259      180    (406,300)  (-294,-200)
	MOVEA 150 600       356       60    ( 10,408)  (-140,-192)
	MOVEA 900 100       485      299    none        -
	MOVEA 300 200       389       60    (137,112)  (-163, -88)

      Every issued count total is exactly right for the belief's delta, and
      every landing is exactly right for a pointer whose true origin is the
      corner while the belief thinks it is at the first target. The scale was
      never wrong. The origin was, by precisely the first target's travel.

THE FIX, two parts, both in the same branch.

  Size the slam through the gain instead of in raw counts. 18 * m_move_step
  is 2160 counts on this tile — 3300 px of travel to cross 1024 — so the
  overshoot, and hence the debt, is an order of magnitude larger than the
  surface. ceil(screen / gain) + one window's margin is the smallest slam
  that still reaches the corner from anywhere: 662 + 120 on X, 459 + 120 on
  Y here. This bounds the debt; it does not remove it, because the arrow may
  start anywhere.

  Defer the travel until the slam has drained. A new pending-home slot holds
  the target; drain_move() releases it as paced travel once the queue is
  empty AND MAME_CTL_HOME_SETTLE seconds have passed since the last issued
  count. That is measurement (2)'s settle, made part of the branch. It costs
  the home settle once per session (or per state load), on the first MOVEA
  only — every later target takes the belief branch and is unaffected.

MAME_CTL_HOME_SETTLE   seconds to let the guest's own mouse accumulator
                       drain after the homing slam before the first target's
                       travel is issued. 0 restores today's behaviour (slam
                       and travel enqueued together).            [0.75]

NOT YET PROVEN ON THE FRAMEBUFFER: written at the stop of a timeboxed run,
from measurements (1)-(4) above but not built or bake-tested. The next stream
builds it into a sandbox out/ and re-runs the five-target table plus a return
to the first target; ±6 px is the bar.

--- a/src/osd/modules/ctlsock/ctlsock.cpp
+++ b/src/osd/modules/ctlsock/ctlsock.cpp
@@ -344,6 +344,9 @@
 		m_move_window = double(env_long("MAME_CTL_MOVE_WINDOW", 40)) / 1e3;
+		m_home_settle = double(env_long("MAME_CTL_HOME_SETTLE", 750)) / 1e3;
+		if (m_home_settle < 0.0 || m_home_settle > 10.0)
+			m_home_settle = 0.75;

--- drain_move: release a deferred post-home travel
@@ -1388,6 +1391,25 @@
 	void drain_move()
 	{
+		// A homing slam does NOT die against the guest's edge clamp: the
+		// clamp is in the card's firmware, downstream of an accumulator
+		// that NETS (a2bus mouse.cpp update_axis(): `m_count += diff`,
+		// drained one unit per MCU port-B read). Travel appended to a
+		// still-negative accumulator is spent unwinding the slam instead
+		// of moving the arrow -- measured on apple2e: the whole of the
+		// first target's travel, at every slam size. Hold the travel until
+		// the queue is empty and the guest has had HOME_SETTLE quiet to
+		// drain, then issue it against a real corner.
+		if (m_home_pend && m_mq.empty()
+				&& (m_last_issue < 0.0
+					|| (emu_now() - m_last_issue) >= m_home_settle))
+		{
+			m_home_pend = false;
+			long const hx = m_home_x, hy = m_home_y;
+			m_home_x = 0;
+			m_home_y = 0;
+			move_paced_travel_px(hx, hy);
+		}
 		if (m_mq.empty())
 			return;

--- movea_target open-loop home branch
@@ -1826,8 +1848,20 @@
 			if (!m_fl_valid)
 			{
-				move_paced(-(18 * m_move_step), -(18 * m_move_step));
-				move_paced_travel_px(x, y);
+				// Size the slam in PIXELS through the gain: 18 * m_move_step
+				// is 2160 counts = ~3300 px on a 1024-wide surface, so the
+				// overshoot -- and the accumulator debt it leaves -- is an
+				// order of magnitude larger than it needs to be. One screen
+				// plus one window's margin reaches the corner from anywhere.
+				long const sx = long(std::ceil(double(m_surf_w) / m_gx)) + m_move_step;
+				long const sy = long(std::ceil(double(m_surf_h) / m_gy)) + m_move_step;
+				move_paced(-sx, -sy);
+				// and hold the travel until that has actually drained
+				// through the device, not merely through our queue
+				m_home_x = x;
+				m_home_y = y;
+				m_home_pend = true;
+				if (m_home_settle <= 0.0)  // opt-out: today's behaviour
+				{
+					m_home_pend = false;
+					move_paced_travel_px(x, y);
+				}
 			}

--- members
@@ -3093,6 +3127,10 @@
 	double m_last_issue = -1.0;              // emu time of the last issued count
+	double m_home_settle = 0.75;             // MAME_CTL_HOME_SETTLE
+	bool m_home_pend = false;                // first-MOVEA travel held for the
+	long m_home_x = 0;                       // homing slam to drain out of the
+	long m_home_y = 0;                       // guest's own accumulator
